  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1110 PM MDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nWhile most of today's activity has died down, we still have a few \nshowers making their way across the eastern plains, and we expect \nthem to continue weakening as they move east. As skies clear up and \nwinds become light behind these showers, we could see some patchy \nfog develop in the northeastern counties for a few hours tonight.\n\nTomorrow and Sunday should both be much quieter weather days, thanks \nto subsidence from an upper-level ridge building over the area \ntonight into tomorrow. Zonal flow aloft from the ridge with some \ndownsloping will bring some breezier conditions to the mountains and \nfoothills in the early morning, with wind gusts of about 20-30 kts \nacross the area and gusts up to 40 kts possible for the higher \nelevations. Dewpoints should drop into the 20s and 30s as the ridge \nmoves into our CWA, and our high temperatures are expected to be in \nthe mid 60s-70s for the mountains and low- to mid-80s for the \nplains. With both subsidence from the ridge and drier conditions at \nthe surface, this weekend should stay dry and mostly sunny, though \nSunday afternoon and evening could see a few showers over the \nmountains.\n\nModels are trending slightly slower with the upper level trough/low \nnext week. This has a few impacts on our potential weather for the\nMonday through Thursday time period: One, the slower trough will \npush our better rain chances to later in the week, lowering our \nprecip chances (and therefore snow chances) for the Monday and \nTuesday time frame. Two, the slower track could allow for the \nremnants of Hurricane Polo to merge with the trough prior to it \nmoving towards our area, which would give us better moisture and \nrain chances for late Tuesday through Wednesday. However, models \nare also trending a bit further south with the low, which will \nkeep the best rain chances to the south/southeast of our forecast \narea. It also looks like the change in the positioning/timing of \nthe upper low has impacted the strength of the cold front late \nMonday into Tuesday. The surface troughing and front are much \nweaker in the recent runs compared to those over the past few \ndays. The weaker front and warmer temperatures will impact our \nsnow chances Monday night/Tuesday morning in the higher terrain. \nSnow chances and amounts for this time period continue to \ndecrease, with chances for at least a dusting of snow lowering \ninto the 10% to 40% range for our higher mountain elevations \n(generally above 12,000ft now). \n\nFor late Tuesday through Thursday, models have been pretty \nconsistent with a swath of heavy rain developing, as moisture \nstreams into the area and the upper low progresses from the SW \nU.S. into the central/south central U.S. However, the placement of\nthis swath varies from run to run. Similar to everything else, it\nwill depend on the evolution of the upper level system and \nsurface low. Unfortunately for us, we look to be on the northern \nperiphery of the higher rain totals, which means that there could \nbe a pretty tight rainfall gradient somewhere over our forecast \narea (or potentially even just to the south of our area, missing \nus), where some people see multiple inches and others just a bit \nfurther north see tenths at most. The placement of this strong \ngradient will likely be the most difficult thing for us to \nforecast, and one of the most impactful parts of the forecast. The\nspeed of the upper level low will determine when the rain chances\nend for us, with some members holding onto rain chances through \nearly Friday, while others have us drying out Thursday.",
